IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `theft-nouns`
IMPORT `theft-s01-06-definition`

-- Theft Act 1968, sections 7 to 14: theft, robbery, burglary and the other
-- offences of the first group, with the penalty each section states. Layer 1.
--
-- Each offence is a yes-or-no rule over the facts that make it up. The penalty is
-- a ceiling ("liable to ... not exceeding"), never a duty to pay or serve, and it
-- is the greatest the section itself states: where a section states a fine it
-- states a LEVEL on the standard scale, and the sum for a level is not in this
-- text, so none is supplied. What a charge under a section amounts to is a rule
-- named "liable ..." that returns an `Outcome of a charge`.
--
-- Words of state of mind in these sections ("dishonestly" in s 13, "intent", "knowing",
-- "believes") are facts the person states; s 2 defines "dishonestly" only for s 1
-- (s 1(3), NOTES.md fork 1).

§ `Sections 7 to 14 - theft, robbery, burglary and related offences`

§§ `Section 7 - theft`

-- src:40 | A person guilty of theft shall on conviction on indictment be liable to imprisonment for a term not exceeding seven years.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 7 (src:40)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 7 penalty for theft` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of years` 7)
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 7 (src:40)
GIVEN t IS A `Theft facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 7 liable on conviction on indictment for theft` t MEANS
    IF `s 1 guilty of theft` t
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 7 penalty for theft`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

§§ `Section 8 - robbery`

-- src:42 | (1) A person is guilty of robbery if he steals, and immediately before or at the time of doing so, and in order to do so, he uses force on any person or puts or seeks to put any person in fear of being then and there subjected to force.
-- src:43 | (2) A person guilty of robbery, or of an assault with intent to rob, shall on conviction on indictment be liable to imprisonment for life.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 8 (src:42-43)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 8(2) penalty for robbery or assault with intent to rob` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS `imprisonment for life`
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 8(1) (src:42)
GIVEN r IS A `Robbery facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 8(1) guilty of robbery` r MEANS
        "steals" ... `s 1 guilty of theft` (r's `the stealing`)
    AND "immediately before or at the time of doing so" ... r's `immediately before or at the time of stealing`
    AND "and in order to do so" ... r's `in order to steal`
    AND "uses force on any person or puts or seeks to put any person in fear of being then and there subjected to force" ...
            (    r's `used force on any person`
             OR  r's `put or sought to put any person in fear of being then and there subjected to force`)

-- "An assault with intent to rob" is not defined in this Act, so that it happened is
-- a fact the person states (NOTES.md, fork 9).
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 8(2) (src:43)
GIVEN r IS A `Robbery facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 8(2) liable on conviction on indictment for robbery or assault with intent to rob` r MEANS
    IF `s 8(1) guilty of robbery` r OR r's `committed an assault with intent to rob`
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 8(2) penalty for robbery or assault with intent to rob`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

§§ `Section 9 - burglary`

-- src:45 | (1) A person is guilty of burglary if—
-- src:46 | (a) he enters any building or part of a building as a trespasser and with intent to commit any such offence as is mentioned in subsection (2) below; or
-- src:47 | (b) having entered any building or part of a building as a trespasser he steals or attempts to steal anything in the building or that part of it or inflicts or attempts to inflict on any person therein any grievous bodily harm.
-- src:48 | (2) The offences referred to in subsection (1)(a) above are offences of stealing anything in the building or part of a building in question, of inflicting on any person therein any grievous bodily harm ... therein, and of doing unlawful damage to the building or anything therein.
-- src:49 | (3) A person guilty of burglary shall on conviction on indictment be liable to imprisonment for a term not exceeding—
-- src:50 | (a) where the offence was committed in respect of a building or part of a building which is a dwelling, fourteen years;
-- src:51 | (b) in any other case, ten years.
-- src:52 | (4) References in subsections (1) and (2) above to a building, and the reference in subsection (3) above to a building which is a dwelling, shall apply also to an inhabited vehicle or vessel, and shall apply to any such vehicle or vessel at times when the person having a habitation in it is not there as well as at times when he is.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 9 (src:45-52)
GIVEN b IS A `Burglary facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 9(1) guilty of burglary` b MEANS
        "(a)" ... `s 9(1)(a) enters as a trespasser with intent to commit one of the offences in subsection (2)` b
    OR  "(b)" ... `s 9(1)(b) having entered as a trespasser, steals or attempts to steal, or inflicts or attempts to inflict grievous bodily harm` b

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 9(1)(a) (src:46)
GIVEN b IS A `Burglary facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 9(1)(a) enters as a trespasser with intent to commit one of the offences in subsection (2)` b MEANS
        `s 9(4) the place is a building` b
    AND b's `entered as a trespasser`
    AND `s 9(2) he intended one of the offences` b

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 9(1)(b) (src:47)
GIVEN b IS A `Burglary facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 9(1)(b) having entered as a trespasser, steals or attempts to steal, or inflicts or attempts to inflict grievous bodily harm` b MEANS
        `s 9(4) the place is a building` b
    AND b's `entered as a trespasser`
    AND (    (    `s 1 guilty of theft` (b's `the stealing after entry`)
              AND b's `the property stolen was in the building or that part of it`)
         OR  b's `after entry he attempted to steal anything in the building or that part of it`
         OR  b's `after entry he inflicted or attempted to inflict grievous bodily harm on a person in it`)

-- s 9(2). The text of the revised version shows dots after "grievous bodily harm"
-- (words repealed); the offences left are stealing, grievous bodily harm and
-- unlawful damage.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 9(2) (src:48)
GIVEN b IS A `Burglary facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 9(2) he intended one of the offences` b MEANS
        b's `at entry he intended to steal anything in it`
    OR  b's `at entry he intended to inflict grievous bodily harm on a person in it`
    OR  b's `at entry he intended to do unlawful damage to it or anything in it`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 9(3)(a) (src:50)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 9(3)(a) penalty for burglary of a dwelling` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of years` 14)
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 9(3)(b) (src:51)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 9(3)(b) penalty for any other burglary` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of years` 10)
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

-- s 9(4). A building includes an inhabited vehicle or vessel, and a dwelling
-- includes one, whether or not the person living in it is there at the time.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 9(4) (src:52)
GIVEN b IS A `Burglary facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 9(4) the place is a building` b MEANS
        b's `the place entered is a building or part of a building`
    OR  b's `the place entered is an inhabited vehicle or vessel`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 9(4) (src:52)
GIVEN b IS A `Burglary facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 9(3)(a) the place is a dwelling` b MEANS
        (    b's `the place entered is a building or part of a building`
         AND b's `the place entered is a dwelling`)
    OR  b's `the place entered is an inhabited vehicle or vessel`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 9(3) (src:49-51)
GIVEN b IS A `Burglary facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 9(3) liable on conviction on indictment for burglary` b MEANS
    IF `s 9(1) guilty of burglary` b
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states`
            (IF `s 9(3)(a) the place is a dwelling` b
             THEN `s 9(3)(a) penalty for burglary of a dwelling`
             ELSE `s 9(3)(b) penalty for any other burglary`)
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

§§ `Section 10 - aggravated burglary`

-- src:54 | (1) A person is guilty of aggravated burglary if he commits any burglary and at the time has with him any firearm or imitation firearm, any weapon of offence, or any explosive; and for this purpose—
-- src:55 | (a) “firearm” includes an airgun or air pistol, and “imitation firearm” means anything which has the appearance of being a firearm, whether capable of being discharged or not; and
-- src:56 | (b) “weapon of offence” means any article made or adapted for use for causing injury to or incapacitating a person, or intended by the person having it with him for such use; and
-- src:57 | (c) “explosive” means any article manufactured for the purpose of producing a practical effect by explosion, or intended by the person having it with him for that purpose.
-- src:58 | (2) A person guilty of aggravated burglary shall on conviction on indictment be liable to imprisonment for life.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 10 (src:54-58)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 10(2) penalty for aggravated burglary` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS `imprisonment for life`
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 10(1) (src:54)
GIVEN a IS AN `Aggravated burglary facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10(1) guilty of aggravated burglary` a MEANS
        "he commits any burglary" ... `s 9(1) guilty of burglary` (a's `the burglary`)
    AND "and at the time has with him" ...
            (    "any firearm or imitation firearm" ... `s 10(1)(a) a firearm or imitation firearm` a
             OR  "any weapon of offence" ... `s 10(1)(b) a weapon of offence` a
             OR  "any explosive" ... `s 10(1)(c) an explosive` a)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 10(1)(a) (src:55)
GIVEN a IS AN `Aggravated burglary facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10(1)(a) a firearm or imitation firearm` a MEANS
        a's `at the time had with him a firearm, including an airgun or air pistol`
    OR  a's `at the time had with him anything with the appearance of a firearm, whether or not capable of being discharged`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 10(1)(b) (src:56)
GIVEN a IS AN `Aggravated burglary facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10(1)(b) a weapon of offence` a MEANS
        a's `at the time had with him an article made or adapted for use for causing injury to or incapacitating a person`
    OR  a's `at the time had with him an article he intended for causing injury to or incapacitating a person`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 10(1)(c) (src:57)
GIVEN a IS AN `Aggravated burglary facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10(1)(c) an explosive` a MEANS
        a's `at the time had with him an article manufactured for the purpose of producing a practical effect by explosion`
    OR  a's `at the time had with him an article he intended for the purpose of producing a practical effect by explosion`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 10(2) (src:58)
GIVEN a IS AN `Aggravated burglary facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 10(2) liable on conviction on indictment for aggravated burglary` a MEANS
    IF `s 10(1) guilty of aggravated burglary` a
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 10(2) penalty for aggravated burglary`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

§§ `Section 11 - removal of articles from places open to the public`

-- src:60 | (1) Subject to subsections (2) and (3) below, where the public have access to a building in order to view the building or part of it, or a collection or part of a collection housed in it, any person who without lawful authority removes from the building or its grounds the whole or part of any article displayed or kept for display to the public in the building or that part of it or in its grounds shall be guilty of an offence.
-- src:61 | For this purpose “collection” includes a collection got together for a temporary purpose, but references in this section to a collection do not apply to a collection made or exhibited for the purpose of effecting sales or other commercial dealings.
-- src:62 | (2) It is immaterial for purposes of subsection (1) above, that the public’s access to a building is limited to a particular period or particular occasion; but where anything removed from a building or its grounds is there otherwise than as forming part of, or being on loan for exhibition with, a collection intended for permanent exhibition to the public, the person removing it does not thereby commit an offence under this section unless he removes it on a day when the public have access to the building as mentioned in subsection (1) above.
-- src:63 | (3) A person does not commit an offence under this section if he believes that he has lawful authority for the removal of the thing in question or that he would have it if the person entitled to give it knew of the removal and the circumstances of it.
-- src:64 | (4) A person guilty of an offence under this section shall, on conviction on indictment, be liable to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 11 (src:60-64)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 11(4) penalty for removing an article from a place open to the public` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of years` 5)
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 11(1) (src:60-61)
GIVEN f IS A `Removal facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 11(1) the public have access to view the building or a collection housed in it` f MEANS
        f's `the public have access to the building in order to view it or part of it`
    OR  (    f's `the public have access in order to view a collection or part of a collection housed in it`
         AND NOT (f's `the collection is made or exhibited for the purpose of effecting sales or other commercial dealings`))

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 11(2) (src:62)
GIVEN f IS A `Removal facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 11(2) the day of removal does not save him` f MEANS
        f's `the article was there as part of, or on loan for exhibition with, a collection intended for permanent exhibition to the public`
    OR  f's `removed it on a day when the public have access to the building`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 11(3) (src:63)
GIVEN f IS A `Removal facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 11(3) he believes he has lawful authority` f MEANS
        f's `believed he had lawful authority for the removal`
    OR  f's `believed he would have lawful authority if the person entitled to give it knew of the removal and the circumstances`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 11(1) to (3) (src:60-63)
GIVEN f IS A `Removal facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 11 guilty of removing an article from a place open to the public` f MEANS
        `s 11(1) the public have access to view the building or a collection housed in it` f
    AND f's `removed from the building or its grounds the whole or part of an article`
    AND f's `the article was displayed or kept for display to the public in the building or that part of it or in its grounds`
    AND NOT (f's `removed it with lawful authority`)
    AND `s 11(2) the day of removal does not save him` f
    AND NOT (`s 11(3) he believes he has lawful authority` f)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 11(4) (src:64)
GIVEN f IS A `Removal facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 11(4) liable on conviction on indictment for removing an article` f MEANS
    IF `s 11 guilty of removing an article from a place open to the public` f
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 11(4) penalty for removing an article from a place open to the public`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

§§ `Section 12 - taking a motor vehicle or other conveyance without authority`

-- src:66 | (1) Subject to subsections (5) and (6) below, a person shall be guilty of an offence if, without having the consent of the owner or other lawful authority, he takes any conveyance for his own or another’s use or, knowing that any conveyance has been taken without such authority, drives it or allows himself to be carried in or on it.
-- src:67 | (2) A person guilty of an offence under subsection (1) above shall be liable on summary conviction to a fine not exceeding level 5 on the standard scale, to imprisonment for a term not exceeding six months, or to both.
-- src:68 | (3) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:69 | (4) If on the trial of an indictment for theft the jury are not satisfied that the accused committed theft, but it is proved that the accused committed an offence under subsection (1) above, the jury may find him guilty of the offence under subsection (1) and if he is found guilty of it, he shall be liable as he would have been liable under subsection (2) above on summary conviction.
-- src:70 | (4A) Proceedings for an offence under subsection (1) above (but not proceedings of a kind falling within subsection (4) above) in relation to a mechanically propelled vehicle—
-- src:71 | (a) shall not be commenced after the end of the period of three years beginning with the day on which the offence was committed; but
-- src:72 | (b) subject to that, may be commenced at any time within the period of six months beginning with the relevant day.
-- src:73 | (4B) In subsection (4A)(b) above “the relevant day” means—
-- src:74 | (a) in the case of a prosecution for an offence under subsection (1) above by a public prosecutor, the day on which sufficient evidence to justify the proceedings came to the knowledge of any person responsible for deciding whether to commence any such prosecution;
-- src:75 | (b) in the case of a prosecution for an offence under subsection (1) above which is commenced by a person other than a public prosecutor after the discontinuance of a prosecution falling within paragraph (a) above which relates to the same facts, the day on which sufficient evidence to justify the proceedings came to the knowledge of the person who has decided to commence the prosecution or (if later) the discontinuance of the other prosecution;
-- src:76 | (c) in the case of any other prosecution for an offence under subsection (1) above, the day on which sufficient evidence to justify the proceedings came to the knowledge of the person who has decided to commence the prosecution.
-- src:77 | (4C) For the purposes of subsection (4A)(b) above a certificate of a person responsible for deciding whether to commence a prosecution of a kind mentioned in subsection (4B)(a) above as to the date on which such evidence as is mentioned in the certificate came to the knowledge of any person responsible for deciding whether to commence any such prosecution shall be conclusive evidence of that fact.
-- src:78 | (5) Subsection (1) above shall not apply in relation to pedal cycles; but, subject to subsection (6) below, a person who, without having the consent of the owner or other lawful authority, takes a pedal cycle for his own or another’s use, or rides a pedal cycle knowing it to have been taken without such authority, shall on summary conviction be liable to a fine not exceeding level 3 on the standard scale.
-- src:79 | (6) A person does not commit an offence under this section by anything done in the belief that he has lawful authority to do it or that he would have the owner’s consent if the owner knew of his doing it and the circumstances of it.
-- src:80 | (7) For purposes of this section—
-- src:81 | (a) “conveyance” means any conveyance constructed or adapted for the carriage of a person or persons whether by land, water or air, except that it does not include a conveyance constructed or adapted for use only under the control of a person not carried in or on it, and “drive” shall be construed accordingly; and
-- src:82 | (b) “owner”, in relation to a conveyance which is the subject of a hiring agreement or hire-purchase agreement, means the person in possession of the conveyance under that agreement.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12 (src:66-82)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 12(2) penalty for taking a conveyance without authority` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on summary conviction`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of months` 6)
    `the fine ceiling` IS (`a fine not exceeding a level on the standard scale` 5)
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS TRUE

-- s 12(7)(a). "Drive" is construed accordingly; it needs no separate rule.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(7)(a) (src:81)
GIVEN c IS A `Conveyance facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(7)(a) it is a conveyance` c MEANS
        c's `constructed or adapted for the carriage of a person or persons by land, water or air`
    AND NOT (c's `constructed or adapted for use only under the control of a person not carried in or on it`)

-- s 12(7)(b). For a hired or hire-purchase conveyance "the owner" is the person
-- in possession under the agreement, not the person who lets it out.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(7)(b) (src:82)
GIVEN c IS A `Conveyance facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(7)(b) the owner consented` c MEANS
    IF c's `it is the subject of a hiring agreement or hire-purchase agreement`
    THEN c's `the person in possession under the hiring or hire-purchase agreement consented`
    ELSE c's `the owner consented`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(1) (src:66)
GIVEN c IS A `Conveyance facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(1) without the consent of the owner or other lawful authority` c MEANS
        NOT (`s 12(7)(b) the owner consented` c)
    AND NOT (c's `he had other lawful authority`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(6) (src:79)
GIVEN c IS A `Conveyance facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(6) he did it in the belief that he had authority or the owner's consent` c MEANS
        c's `believed he had lawful authority to do what he did`
    OR  c's `believed he would have the owner's consent if the owner knew of his doing it and the circumstances`

-- s 12(1). The words "without having the consent of the owner or other lawful
-- authority" are read as governing both limbs (NOTES.md, fork 10).
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(1) (src:66)
GIVEN c IS A `Conveyance facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(1) guilty of taking a conveyance without authority` c MEANS
        `s 12(7)(a) it is a conveyance` c
    AND NOT (c's `it is a pedal cycle`)
    AND `s 12(1) without the consent of the owner or other lawful authority` c
    AND NOT (`s 12(6) he did it in the belief that he had authority or the owner's consent` c)
    AND (    c's `took it for his own or another's use`
         OR  (    c's `knew it had been taken without such consent or authority`
              AND (    c's `drove it`
                   OR  c's `allowed himself to be carried in or on it`)))

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(2) (src:67)
GIVEN c IS A `Conveyance facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 12(2) liable on summary conviction for taking a conveyance without authority` c MEANS
    IF `s 12(1) guilty of taking a conveyance without authority` c
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 12(2) penalty for taking a conveyance without authority`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

-- s 12(3) is shown as dots in this text and says nothing.

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(4) (src:69)
GIVEN v IS AN `Alternative verdict facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(4) the jury may find him guilty of taking a conveyance without authority` v MEANS
        v's `tried on indictment for theft`
    AND v's `the jury are not satisfied the accused committed theft`
    AND v's `it is proved the accused committed an offence under section 12(1)`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(4) (src:69)
GIVEN v IS AN `Alternative verdict facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 12(4) liable as under subsection (2) on a verdict returned on a charge of theft` v MEANS
    IF `s 12(4) the jury may find him guilty of taking a conveyance without authority` v
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 12(2) penalty for taking a conveyance without authority`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

-- s 12(4B). "The relevant day". Where an unrelated prosecutor takes over after a
-- public prosecution was discontinued, the day is the later of two dates; if the
-- discontinuance date is missing the evidence date is used (NOTES.md, fork 11).
-- Under s 12(4C) a certificate from the public prosecutor's side of the date
-- evidence came to knowledge is conclusive, so the date asked for is the certified
-- one where there is a certificate.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(4B) (src:73-76)
GIVEN t IS A `Time limit facts`
GIVETH A DATE
`s 12(4B) the relevant day` t MEANS
    CONSIDER t's `who is prosecuting`
        WHEN `a public prosecutor` THEN
            t's `the date sufficient evidence to justify the proceedings came to the knowledge of the person section 12(4B) names`
        WHEN `another person, after a public prosecution on the same facts was discontinued` THEN
            CONSIDER t's `the date the earlier public prosecution was discontinued`
                WHEN JUST discontinued THEN
                    IF discontinued AT LEAST (t's `the date sufficient evidence to justify the proceedings came to the knowledge of the person section 12(4B) names`)
                    THEN discontinued
                    ELSE t's `the date sufficient evidence to justify the proceedings came to the knowledge of the person section 12(4B) names`
                WHEN NOTHING THEN
                    t's `the date sufficient evidence to justify the proceedings came to the knowledge of the person section 12(4B) names`
        WHEN `another person, in any other case` THEN
            t's `the date sufficient evidence to justify the proceedings came to the knowledge of the person section 12(4B) names`

-- s 12(4A)(a). The period of three years beginning with the day of the offence
-- ends the day before the third anniversary.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(4A)(a) (src:71)
GIVEN t IS A `Time limit facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(4A)(a) the three years have not ended` t MEANS
    t's `the date the proceedings were commenced` LESS THAN (`add years` (t's `the date the offence was committed`) 3)

-- s 12(4A)(b). The period of six months beginning with the relevant day ends the
-- day before the same day six months later. Only the upper end is read: nothing in
-- the text bars proceedings begun before the relevant day (NOTES.md, fork 12).
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(4A)(b) (src:72)
GIVEN t IS A `Time limit facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(4A)(b) within six months of the relevant day` t MEANS
    t's `the date the proceedings were commenced` LESS THAN (`add months` (`s 12(4B) the relevant day` t) 6)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(4A) (src:70-72)
GIVEN t IS A `Time limit facts`
GIVETH A `Time limit position`
`s 12(4A) the time limit for proceedings` t MEANS
    IF (NOT (t's `the vehicle is mechanically propelled`)) OR t's `the proceedings are of the kind described in section 12(4)`
    THEN `section 12(4A) sets no limit on these proceedings`
    ELSE IF `s 12(4A)(a) the three years have not ended` t AND `s 12(4A)(b) within six months of the relevant day` t
         THEN `section 12(4A) allows these proceedings to be commenced`
         ELSE `section 12(4A) bars these proceedings`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(5) (src:78)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 12(5) penalty for taking or riding a pedal cycle without authority` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on summary conviction`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS `the section states no imprisonment`
    `the fine ceiling` IS (`a fine not exceeding a level on the standard scale` 3)
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(5) (src:78)
GIVEN c IS A `Conveyance facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12(5) guilty of taking or riding a pedal cycle without authority` c MEANS
        c's `it is a pedal cycle`
    AND `s 12(1) without the consent of the owner or other lawful authority` c
    AND NOT (`s 12(6) he did it in the belief that he had authority or the owner's consent` c)
    AND (    c's `took it for his own or another's use`
         OR  (    c's `rode it`
              AND c's `knew it had been taken without such consent or authority`))

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12(5) (src:78)
GIVEN c IS A `Conveyance facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 12(5) liable on summary conviction for taking or riding a pedal cycle without authority` c MEANS
    IF `s 12(5) guilty of taking or riding a pedal cycle without authority` c
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 12(5) penalty for taking or riding a pedal cycle without authority`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

§§ `Section 12A - aggravated vehicle-taking`

-- src:84 | (1) Subject to subsection (3) below, a person is guilty of aggravated taking of a vehicle if—
-- src:85 | (a) he commits an offence under section 12(1) above (in this section referred to as a “basic offence”) in relation to a mechanically propelled vehicle; and
-- src:86 | (b) it is proved that, at any time after the vehicle was unlawfully taken (whether by him or another) and before it was recovered, the vehicle was driven, or injury or damage was caused, in one or more of the circumstances set out in paragraphs (a) to (d) of subsection (2) below.
-- src:87 | (2) The circumstances referred to in subsection (1)(b) above are—
-- src:88 | (a) that the vehicle was driven dangerously on a road or other public place;
-- src:89 | (b) that, owing to the driving of the vehicle, an accident occurred by which injury was caused to any person;
-- src:90 | (c) that, owing to the driving of the vehicle, an accident occurred by which damage was caused to any property, other than the vehicle;
-- src:91 | (d) that damage was caused to the vehicle.
-- src:92 | (3) A person is not guilty of an offence under this section if he proves that, as regards any such proven driving, injury or damage as is referred to in subsection (1)(b) above, either—
-- src:93 | (a) the driving, accident or damage referred to in subsection (2) above occurred before he committed the basic offence; or
-- src:94 | (b) he was neither in nor on nor in the immediate vicinity of the vehicle when that driving, accident or damage occurred.
-- src:95 | (4) A person guilty of an offence under this section shall be liable on conviction on indictment to imprisonment for a term not exceeding two years or, if it is proved that, in circumstances falling within subsection (2)(b) above, the accident caused the death of the person concerned, fourteen years.
-- src:96 | (5) If a person who is charged with an offence under this section is found not guilty of that offence but it is proved that he committed a basic offence, he may be convicted of the basic offence.
-- src:97 | (6) If by virtue of subsection (5) above a person is convicted of a basic offence before the Crown Court, that court shall have the same powers and duties as a magistrates’ court would have had on convicting him of such an offence.
-- src:98 | (7) For the purposes of this section a vehicle is driven dangerously if—
-- src:99 | (a) it is driven in a way which falls far below what would be expected of a competent and careful driver; and
-- src:100 | (b) it would be obvious to a competent and careful driver that driving the vehicle in that way would be dangerous.
-- src:101 | (8) For the purposes of this section a vehicle is recovered when it is restored to its owner or to other lawful possession or custody; and in this subsection “owner” has the same meaning as in section 12 above.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A (src:84-101)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 12A(4) penalty where no death was caused` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of years` 2)
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(4) (src:95)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 12A(4) penalty where an accident in circumstance (b) caused death` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of years` 14)
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

-- s 12A(7). "Driven dangerously".
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(7) (src:98-100)
GIVEN d IS A `Dangerous driving facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12A(7) driven dangerously` d MEANS
        d's `it was driven in a way which falls far below what would be expected of a competent and careful driver`
    AND d's `it would be obvious to a competent and careful driver that driving in that way would be dangerous`

-- s 12A(1)(b) and (8). A circumstance counts only if it happened after the vehicle
-- was unlawfully taken (by him or another) and before it was recovered, that is
-- restored to its owner or to other lawful possession or custody.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(1)(b) (src:86)
GIVEN c IS A `Vehicle-taking circumstance`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12A(1)(b) the circumstance occurred between the taking and the recovery` c MEANS
        c's `the driving, accident or damage happened`
    AND c's `by then the vehicle had been unlawfully taken`
    AND NOT (c's `by then the vehicle had been recovered, that is restored to its owner or to other lawful possession or custody`)

-- s 12A(3). The accused must prove one of the two matters; they are asked as
-- whether he can prove them.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(3) (src:92-94)
GIVEN c IS A `Vehicle-taking circumstance`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12A(3) he proves one of the two defences for the circumstance` c MEANS
        c's `it happened before he committed the basic offence`
    OR  c's `he was neither in nor on nor in the immediate vicinity of the vehicle when it happened`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(2)(a) (src:88)
GIVEN f IS AN `Aggravated vehicle-taking facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12A(2)(a) the vehicle was driven dangerously on a road or other public place` f MEANS
        `s 12A(1)(b) the circumstance occurred between the taking and the recovery` (f's `circumstance (a), driving dangerously`)
    AND (f's `the dangerous driving`)'s `the vehicle was driven on a road or other public place`
    AND `s 12A(7) driven dangerously` (f's `the dangerous driving`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(2)(b) (src:89)
GIVEN f IS AN `Aggravated vehicle-taking facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12A(2)(b) an accident owing to the driving injured a person` f MEANS
    `s 12A(1)(b) the circumstance occurred between the taking and the recovery` (f's `circumstance (b), an accident owing to the driving injured a person`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(2)(c) (src:90)
GIVEN f IS AN `Aggravated vehicle-taking facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12A(2)(c) an accident owing to the driving damaged property other than the vehicle` f MEANS
    `s 12A(1)(b) the circumstance occurred between the taking and the recovery` (f's `circumstance (c), an accident owing to the driving damaged property other than the vehicle`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(2)(d) (src:91)
GIVEN f IS AN `Aggravated vehicle-taking facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12A(2)(d) damage was caused to the vehicle` f MEANS
    `s 12A(1)(b) the circumstance occurred between the taking and the recovery` (f's `circumstance (d), damage was caused to the vehicle`)

-- s 12A(1) with (3). He is guilty if the basic offence was in relation to a
-- mechanically propelled vehicle and at least one proven circumstance is not
-- covered by a defence he proves (NOTES.md, fork 6).
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(1) (src:84-86)
GIVEN f IS AN `Aggravated vehicle-taking facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12A(1) guilty of aggravated taking of a vehicle` f MEANS
        "he commits an offence under section 12(1) above" ... `s 12(1) guilty of taking a conveyance without authority` (f's `the basic offence`)
    AND "in relation to a mechanically propelled vehicle" ... f's `the vehicle he took is mechanically propelled`
    AND "it is proved that ... in one or more of the circumstances, and (subsection (3)) he does not prove a defence for it" ...
            (    (    `s 12A(2)(a) the vehicle was driven dangerously on a road or other public place` f
                  AND NOT (`s 12A(3) he proves one of the two defences for the circumstance` (f's `circumstance (a), driving dangerously`)))
             OR  (    `s 12A(2)(b) an accident owing to the driving injured a person` f
                  AND NOT (`s 12A(3) he proves one of the two defences for the circumstance` (f's `circumstance (b), an accident owing to the driving injured a person`)))
             OR  (    `s 12A(2)(c) an accident owing to the driving damaged property other than the vehicle` f
                  AND NOT (`s 12A(3) he proves one of the two defences for the circumstance` (f's `circumstance (c), an accident owing to the driving damaged property other than the vehicle`)))
             OR  (    `s 12A(2)(d) damage was caused to the vehicle` f
                  AND NOT (`s 12A(3) he proves one of the two defences for the circumstance` (f's `circumstance (d), damage was caused to the vehicle`))))

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(4) (src:95)
GIVEN f IS AN `Aggravated vehicle-taking facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 12A(4) liable on conviction on indictment for aggravated taking of a vehicle` f MEANS
    IF `s 12A(1) guilty of aggravated taking of a vehicle` f
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states`
            (IF f's `the accident in circumstance (b) caused the death of the person concerned` AND `s 12A(2)(b) an accident owing to the driving injured a person` f
             THEN `s 12A(4) penalty where an accident in circumstance (b) caused death`
             ELSE `s 12A(4) penalty where no death was caused`)
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(5) (src:96)
GIVEN v IS AN `Aggravated vehicle-taking alternative verdict facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12A(5) he may be convicted of the basic offence` v MEANS
        v's `charged with an offence under section 12A`
    AND v's `found not guilty of the offence under section 12A`
    AND v's `it is proved he committed the basic offence`

-- s 12A(6). The Crown Court has the powers of a magistrates' court on convicting
-- of the basic offence, so the greatest penalty is the one in s 12(2).
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 12A(6) (src:97)
GIVEN v IS AN `Aggravated vehicle-taking alternative verdict facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 12A(6) liable as on summary conviction for the basic offence` v MEANS
    IF `s 12A(5) he may be convicted of the basic offence` v
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 12(2) penalty for taking a conveyance without authority`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

§§ `Section 13 - abstracting of electricity`

-- src:103 | A person who dishonestly uses without due authority, or dishonestly causes to be wasted or diverted, any electricity shall on conviction on indictment be liable to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 13 (src:103)
GIVETH A `Penalty stated by the Act`
`s 13 penalty for abstracting electricity` MEANS `Penalty stated by the Act` WITH
    `how convicted` IS `on conviction on indictment`
    `the imprisonment ceiling` IS (`imprisonment for a term not exceeding a number of years` 5)
    `the fine ceiling` IS `the section states no fine`
    `a fine and imprisonment may be imposed together` IS FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 13 (src:103)
GIVEN e IS AN `Electricity facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13 guilty of abstracting electricity` e MEANS
        e's `dishonest in dealing with the electricity`
    AND (    e's `used electricity without due authority`
         OR  e's `caused electricity to be wasted`
         OR  e's `caused electricity to be diverted`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 13 (src:103)
GIVEN e IS AN `Electricity facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 13 liable on conviction on indictment for abstracting electricity` e MEANS
    IF `s 13 guilty of abstracting electricity` e
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 13 penalty for abstracting electricity`
    ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

§§ `Section 14 - thefts from mails outside England and Wales`

-- src:105 | (1) Where a person—
-- src:106 | (a) steals or attempts to steal any mail bag or postal packet in the course of transmission as such between places in different jurisdictions in the British postal area, or any of the contents of such a mail bag or postal packet; or
-- src:107 | (b) in stealing or with intent to steal any such mail bag or postal packet or any of its contents, commits any robbery, attempted robbery or assault with intent to rob;
-- src:108 | then, notwithstanding that he does so outside England and Wales, he shall be guilty of committing or attempting to commit the offence against this Act as if he had done so in England or Wales, and he shall accordingly be liable to be prosecuted, tried and punished in England and Wales without proof that the offence was committed there.
-- src:109 | (2) In subsection (1) above the reference to different jurisdictions in the British postal area is to be construed as referring to the several jurisdictions of England and Wales, of Scotland, of Northern Ireland, of the Isle of Man and of the Channel Islands.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 14 (src:105-109)
GIVEN j IS A `Jurisdiction`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 14(2) the jurisdiction is one of the British postal area` j MEANS
    CONSIDER j
        WHEN `England and Wales` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `Scotland` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `Northern Ireland` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `the Isle of Man` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `the Channel Islands` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `outside the British postal area` THEN FALSE

-- s 14(1)(a), the words "between places in different jurisdictions in the British
-- postal area", with s 14(2).
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 14(2) (src:109)
GIVEN m IS A `Mail facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 14(2) transmitted between different jurisdictions in the British postal area` m MEANS
        `s 14(2) the jurisdiction is one of the British postal area` (m's `transmitted from`)
    AND `s 14(2) the jurisdiction is one of the British postal area` (m's `transmitted to`)
    AND NOT (m's `transmitted from` EQUALS m's `transmitted to`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 14(1) (src:106)
GIVEN m IS A `Mail facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 14(1) the thing is mail in the course of transmission between such places` m MEANS
        m's `the thing is a mail bag, a postal packet, or any of the contents of either`
    AND m's `it was in the course of transmission as such`
    AND `s 14(2) transmitted between different jurisdictions in the British postal area` m

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 14(1)(a) (src:106)
GIVEN m IS A `Mail facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 14(1)(a) steals or attempts to steal such mail` m MEANS
        `s 14(1) the thing is mail in the course of transmission between such places` m
    AND (    `s 1 guilty of theft` ((m's `the robbery`)'s `the stealing`)
         OR  m's `attempted to steal`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 14(1)(b) (src:107)
GIVEN m IS A `Mail facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 14(1)(b) commits robbery, attempted robbery or assault with intent to rob in stealing such mail` m MEANS
        `s 14(1) the thing is mail in the course of transmission between such places` m
    AND (    `s 8(1) guilty of robbery` (m's `the robbery`)
         OR  m's `in stealing or with intent to steal it, attempted robbery`
         OR  (m's `the robbery`)'s `committed an assault with intent to rob`)

-- s 14(1). Whether he does it in or outside England and Wales makes no difference
-- to the answer, so `did it outside England and Wales` is read by no rule.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 14(1) (src:105-108)
GIVEN m IS A `Mail facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 14(1) guilty of the offence as if he had done it in England or Wales` m MEANS
        `s 14(1)(a) steals or attempts to steal such mail` m
    OR  `s 14(1)(b) commits robbery, attempted robbery or assault with intent to rob in stealing such mail` m

-- s 14(1), "prosecuted, tried and punished" as for the offence against this Act:
-- robbery or assault with intent to rob under s 8(2), theft under s 7. For an
-- attempt, this Act states no penalty.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 14(1) (src:108)
GIVEN m IS A `Mail facts`
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
`s 14(1) punishable in England and Wales as the offence he committed` m MEANS
    IF `s 14(1)(b) commits robbery, attempted robbery or assault with intent to rob in stealing such mail` m
       AND (    `s 8(1) guilty of robbery` (m's `the robbery`)
            OR  (m's `the robbery`)'s `committed an assault with intent to rob`)
    THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 8(2) penalty for robbery or assault with intent to rob`
    ELSE IF `s 14(1)(a) steals or attempts to steal such mail` m AND `s 1 guilty of theft` ((m's `the robbery`)'s `the stealing`)
         THEN `an offence, liable to the penalty the section states` `s 7 penalty for theft`
         ELSE IF `s 14(1) guilty of the offence as if he had done it in England or Wales` m
              THEN `an offence, but the text states no penalty for it`
              ELSE `no offence under this section on these facts`

-- s 14(3) is shown as dots in this text and says nothing.
